// Practice quiz: String immutability
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/classes-and-objects/string-immutability

::o1 strip::String s \= "  hi  "; s.strip(); What is s.length() now? {
=6#Correct. strip returns a new String; s still refers to the original six characters.
~2#Not quite. That is the length of the returned value, which was thrown away.
~4#Not quite. strip removes leading and trailing spaces together, and its result was not kept anyway.
~A compile error#Not quite. Calling a method and ignoring its result compiles.
}

::o1 replace::String t \= "cat"; String u \= t.replace("c", "b"); What do t and u hold? {
~Both are "bat"#Not quite. t still refers to the original String.
=t is "cat" and u is "bat"#Correct. replace builds a new String and leaves t unchanged.
~Both are "cat"#Not quite. u holds the new value returned by replace.
~t is "bat" and u is "cat"#Not quite. The original is never changed; the new value is u.
}

::o2 why builder::A loop runs 10,000 times and adds a piece of text each pass. Why use a StringBuilder instead of line \= line + piece? {
~+ is not allowed inside a loop#Not quite. It compiles; it is just wasteful.
~StringBuilder makes the String immutable#Not quite. StringBuilder is the changeable one; toString() makes the final String.
~The two are equally fast#Not quite. Each + inside the loop copies the whole text built so far.
=+ makes a new String and copies all the old characters every pass; a StringBuilder appends to one buffer#Correct. The copying grows with the text, so + gets slower and slower.
}

::o2 chained append::StringBuilder sb \= new StringBuilder("ab"); sb.append("c").append(1); What is sb.toString()? {
~"ab"#Not quite. A StringBuilder changes in place; nothing needs to be assigned back.
~"abc"#Not quite. The second append adds the 1 as text.
="abc1"#Correct. append changes the builder and returns it, so calls chain in order.
~A compile error, because 1 is an int#Not quite. append accepts an int and adds its digits.
}

::o3 shared safely::Two variables refer to the same String object. Can a method called through one of them change what the other sees? {
~Yes, toUpperCase changes it for both#Not quite. toUpperCase returns a new String and leaves the shared one alone.
=No; no String method changes the object, so both always see the same text#Correct. That is why Strings can be shared without copying.
~Yes, any method with a side effect#Not quite. String has no methods that change its characters.
~Only if one of the variables is final#Not quite. final is about the variable, not the object, and is not needed here.
}

::o3 map key::Why is a String safe to use as a key in a HashMap? {
~Strings are always short#Not quite. Length has nothing to do with it.
~HashMap copies every String key#Not quite. It stores the reference; immutability is what keeps it safe.
=Its content can never change, so its hash code stays the same after it is stored#Correct. A key that changed after being stored could no longer be found.
~Only String may be used as a key#Not quite. Many types can be keys; immutable ones are the safe choice.
}
